From a7557102e969f782738b7f2a05a1fc30dcbeca3e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fountain5405 <Fountain5405@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 13:00:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/9] fcmps: verify with the constraints folded into one as
 they are produced

Why: Fcmp::verify stored every constraint of both circuits before forming
the statements. For many-input proofs that is most of the verifier's working
set, about 200 MB per batch for 128-input proofs on stressnet, held by every
concurrently verifying thread.

ArithmeticCircuitStatement::verify weights constraint k by z^(k+1). Handing it
the single constraint sum(z^k c_k) therefore verifies against the same
weighted combination as handing it every constraint, without storing them.
fcmps builds the circuits three times from the same transcript state:
discarding; folding c1 with its z, peeked from the transcript; folding c2.
It checks that each rebuild performed the same multiplications, constraints
and transcript operations. generalized-bulletproofs gains only
VerifierTranscript: Clone and the additive peek_z; verify itself is
unchanged.

Safety:
- Verification only. The prover's circuits still store their constraints
  (Constraints::Stored), so proving is unchanged.
- No line of ArithmeticCircuitStatement::verify is removed or changed, and
  the audited verify still runs on the real transcript for both proofs.
- Any rebuild mismatch returns an error, so a disagreement rejects the
  proof; it cannot accept one.
- Folding refuses circuits with Pedersen commitments
  (FoldedWithPedersenCommitments): the statement's check that each is
  constrained needs the individual constraints.
- The folded constraint keeps every index any constraint referenced, even
  with weight zero, so the statement bounds-checks the same highest indices.
- Verification handles public data only, so variable-time arithmetic is
  acceptable here, as in the existing verifier.

Tests (fcmps/src/tests.rs, +307 lines):
- test_folded_verify_matches_stored and
  test_folded_verify_matches_stored_on_tampered_proofs: the folded verifier
  against the stored one (kept under #[cfg(test)] as Fcmp::verify_stored),
  on honest, tampered and randomly mutated proofs, comparing the batch
  verifiers' contents and verdicts;
- test_discarded_and_folded_circuit_statements;
- verify_memory_benchmark.

Measured at the node with the next commit and the monerod commits, on
a 12-core Ryzen 9 3900X (16 GB RAM, SATA SSD), against v0.19.0.0-beta.3.1:
- stressnet blocks 3,105,401-3,105,444: peak anon -70.4%, against -47.7%
  for the monerod commits alone;
- smallest cgroup memory limit that completes those blocks: 1,765 MB,
  against 3,000 MB for the monerod commits alone and 5,562 MB for stock;
- small-transaction blocks: wall +7.9%, against +0.2% for the monerod
  commits alone. The rebuilds cost time on small proofs.
- The negative differential test (blocks with mutated proofs against
  stock's verdicts) agreed in every case at two heights.

